22
00:01:09,333 --> 00:01:11,523
Let's start with the first part:

23
00:01:11,523 --> 00:01:13,866
ASTC in mobile game development.

24
00:01:16,623 --> 00:01:18,370
ASTC stands for:

25
00:01:18,370 --> 00:01:21,063
"adaptive scalable texture compression"

26
00:01:21,063 --> 00:01:23,943
It is a texture compression
algorithm developed

27
00:01:23,943 --> 00:01:27,483
by ARM and AMD due to its advantages

28
00:01:27,483 --> 00:01:30,663
in compression ratio
and compression quality.

29
00:01:30,663 --> 00:01:33,546
It is now widely used in
mobile game development.

30
00:01:35,943 --> 00:01:38,073
We know tech resources usually take

31
00:01:38,073 --> 00:01:40,923
up a large amount of computing resources

32
00:01:40,923 --> 00:01:44,166
and that's what today's
mobile devices shut off.

33
00:01:45,033 --> 00:01:48,903
That's why most mobile games
use compressed textures.

34
00:01:48,903 --> 00:01:52,113
Using compressed textures
have a lot of benefits.

35
00:01:52,113 --> 00:01:55,263
For example, you will
get smaller game assets,

36
00:01:55,263 --> 00:01:56,523
faster I/O load

37
00:01:56,523 --> 00:01:58,266
and a smaller memory footprint.

38
00:01:59,223 --> 00:02:00,752
Today's GPU also

39
00:02:00,752 --> 00:02:03,513
are apart of several types
of compressed texture format

40
00:02:03,513 --> 00:02:05,343
which makes our job even simpler

41
00:02:05,343 --> 00:02:09,123
because we don't have to
decompress the texture ourself.

42
00:02:09,123 --> 00:02:14,016
So how well is ASTC
supported by current devices?

43
00:02:15,453 --> 00:02:18,003
Here is the hardware support for ASTC

44
00:02:18,003 --> 00:02:19,893
on different platforms.

45
00:02:19,893 --> 00:02:22,653
Generally if you are
targeting devices that support

46
00:02:22,653 --> 00:02:26,406
OpenGL ES 3.1, then you should
not be worried about that.

47
00:02:30,123 --> 00:02:33,513
There are many other compression
format on mobile devices.

48
00:02:33,513 --> 00:02:34,833
The following two images are

49
00:02:34,833 --> 00:02:39,513
from the paper submitted
by the ASTC proposals.

50
00:02:39,513 --> 00:02:41,523
The blue one represents ASTC

51
00:02:41,523 --> 00:02:45,453
and the rest represent other
compression algorithms.

52
00:02:45,453 --> 00:02:49,413
The vertical X axis represent
quality of compress texture.

53
00:02:49,413 --> 00:02:50,673
The higher the better.

54
00:02:50,673 --> 00:02:53,673
And as you can see, ASTC is the best one

55
00:02:53,673 --> 00:02:58,533
in almost all cases like power,
we are texture compression.

56
00:02:58,533 --> 00:03:02,463
ASTC also provide us with
different compression ratio

57
00:03:02,463 --> 00:03:05,523
which is controlled by block size.

58
00:03:05,523 --> 00:03:07,773
ASTC eight by eight is two bit

59
00:03:07,773 --> 00:03:12,773
per pixel and ASTC six
by six is 3.56 per pixel,

60
00:03:14,163 --> 00:03:15,663
et cetera.

61
00:03:15,663 --> 00:03:18,123
The compression quality is usually made

62
00:03:18,123 --> 00:03:22,053
by PSNR, which stand for peaks,

63
00:03:22,053 --> 00:03:24,453
Peak signal-to-noise ratio

64
00:03:24,453 --> 00:03:26,343
it it calculate the difference

65
00:03:26,343 --> 00:03:28,956
between the original image
and the compressed one.

66
00:03:30,903 --> 00:03:33,123
The arrows are averaged for size

67
00:03:33,123 --> 00:03:36,726
so image or different sizes
can show the same measurement.

68
00:03:37,593 --> 00:03:41,013
Larger PSNR values indicate
bad quality, but in

69
00:03:41,013 --> 00:03:45,303
reality we do not need to pursue
very big large PSNR values

70
00:03:45,303 --> 00:03:49,773
because when the PSNR value
is greater than 38 decimal,

71
00:03:49,773 --> 00:03:52,353
it is difficult to follow the human eye to

72
00:03:52,353 --> 00:03:53,403
catch the difference.

73
00:03:57,183 --> 00:03:59,883
To better understand ASTC let's start

74
00:03:59,883 --> 00:04:02,973
with block-based compression algorithm.

75
00:04:02,973 --> 00:04:05,874
As you can see from the illustration image

76
00:04:05,874 --> 00:04:08,493
block-based compression algorithms do not

77
00:04:08,493 --> 00:04:13,086
stop pixels RGB gradient
directly instead, sorry.

78
00:04:14,246 --> 00:04:16,263
Instead it tries to find end points

79
00:04:16,263 --> 00:04:20,733
in your given block and uses
end points to integrate.

80
00:04:20,733 --> 00:04:22,413
So for most of the pixels

81
00:04:22,413 --> 00:04:25,503
within your block only the
greater value needs to be stored

82
00:04:25,503 --> 00:04:28,293
and this will save a lot of space.

83
00:04:28,293 --> 00:04:30,543
ASTC is no different from that and

84
00:04:30,543 --> 00:04:32,763
besides this it provides
different block size

85
00:04:32,763 --> 00:04:37,763
options which makes the
application or ASTC quite flexible.

86
00:04:41,433 --> 00:04:44,763
Different block size means
different compression ratio

87
00:04:44,763 --> 00:04:48,033
because no matter which
block size option is selected

88
00:04:48,033 --> 00:04:51,213
all pixels or values within
that block will be compressed

89
00:04:51,213 --> 00:04:55,563
into a fixed 128 bit representation.

90
00:04:55,563 --> 00:04:58,563
So the largest block
size is, the higher the

91
00:04:58,563 --> 00:05:00,033
compression ratio is as
you can see from the chart.

92
00:05:00,033 --> 00:05:02,583
compression ratio is as
you can see from the chart.

93
00:05:02,583 --> 00:05:06,933
ASTC four by fours bit
rate is almost 10 times

94
00:05:06,933 --> 00:05:09,933
what ASTC eight by eight is

95
00:05:09,933 --> 00:05:10,766
It is no doubt

96
00:05:10,766 --> 00:05:14,163
that smaller block size will
bring better real quality

97
00:05:14,163 --> 00:05:16,213
but let's go through true examples first.

98
00:05:19,053 --> 00:05:20,643
Let's look at an example.

99
00:05:20,643 --> 00:05:24,663
As you can see, the left most
texture is the original one

100
00:05:24,663 --> 00:05:29,643
which is 128 by 128 in size.

101
00:05:29,643 --> 00:05:33,983
Following textures were
generated using ASTC algorithms

102
00:05:33,983 --> 00:05:36,253
with different block size set

103
00:05:37,533 --> 00:05:39,033
from left to right

104
00:05:39,033 --> 00:05:42,093
from four by four to 12 by 12

105
00:05:42,093 --> 00:05:46,053
the size of compressed image
drops from 17 kilobytes

106
00:05:46,053 --> 00:05:48,093
to two kilobytes.

107
00:05:48,093 --> 00:05:52,053
And as as we have mentioned
before, when PSNR value jobs

108
00:05:52,053 --> 00:05:56,706
below 30 decimal, the artifacts
become obvious to human.

109
00:05:58,983 --> 00:06:00,723
You can clearly see the brightness

110
00:06:00,723 --> 00:06:05,403
or textures compressed using
eight by eight or 12 by 12.

111
00:06:05,403 --> 00:06:07,386
Let's look at another example.

112
00:06:10,773 --> 00:06:14,043
We change the texture with less
detail, less straight lines

113
00:06:14,043 --> 00:06:17,523
as you can see, or other
settings remain the same

114
00:06:17,523 --> 00:06:20,103
but the result is quite different.

115
00:06:20,103 --> 00:06:21,393
One thing that is the same

116
00:06:21,393 --> 00:06:25,562
as the previous example is
that the drop of texture size

117
00:06:25,562 --> 00:06:27,996
but the drop or PSNR value is different.

118
00:06:29,703 --> 00:06:33,603
When we use ASTC 12 by 12,
the PSNR value still drops

119
00:06:33,603 --> 00:06:37,203
but it is still way above 30 decimal

120
00:06:37,203 --> 00:06:39,893
which means means to human perception,

121
00:06:39,893 --> 00:06:43,183
12 by 12 is almost no
different from four by four

122
00:06:44,253 --> 00:06:46,866
which requires 10 times
all the space needed.

123
00:06:50,103 --> 00:06:52,023
Let's make a quick summary here.

124
00:06:52,023 --> 00:06:54,393
Different block sizes
have great differences

125
00:06:54,393 --> 00:06:58,083
in compression quality
and the compression rate.

126
00:06:58,083 --> 00:07:01,173
As ASTC supports quite a lot of options.

127
00:07:01,173 --> 00:07:04,623
How should I select the
most satisfying block size

128
00:07:04,623 --> 00:07:06,126
for architectural use?

129
00:07:09,243 --> 00:07:11,133
Let's take Unity for example.

130
00:07:11,133 --> 00:07:14,846
In Unity it's suggest six by
six for normal quality, eight

131
00:07:14,846 --> 00:07:19,846
by eight for low quality and
four by four for high quality.

132
00:07:20,823 --> 00:07:21,903
Here's an example

133
00:07:21,903 --> 00:07:25,656
the suggested block size
for texture In Unity.

134
00:07:27,723 --> 00:07:29,846
If the default value cannot satisfy your

135
00:07:29,846 --> 00:07:32,556
quality requirement,
our storage requirement

136
00:07:32,556 --> 00:07:35,736
you can manually override
the default block size.

137
00:07:37,803 --> 00:07:41,733
So, we have two ways to
choose block size in Unity.

138
00:07:41,733 --> 00:07:44,733
Let's first consider
using the default value

139
00:07:44,733 --> 00:07:48,093
recall the previous
example. For this image

140
00:07:48,093 --> 00:07:50,313
the best choice should be 12 by 12

141
00:07:50,313 --> 00:07:53,673
because it provides us
with the smallest storage

142
00:07:53,673 --> 00:07:56,226
and it is no difference to human eyes.

143
00:07:57,093 --> 00:07:59,613
Even for this small single texture,

144
00:07:59,613 --> 00:08:04,053
you will have to pay about 3,300 kilobytes

145
00:08:04,053 --> 00:08:06,423
for almost no gain in real quality

146
00:08:06,423 --> 00:08:09,006
if you use the default six by six option.

147
00:08:09,873 --> 00:08:11,842
If we have a lot of texture like this

148
00:08:11,842 --> 00:08:14,223
which is most likely to be the case

149
00:08:14,223 --> 00:08:15,726
we will waste more space.

150
00:08:18,813 --> 00:08:21,993
On the other hand, if you
want to manually override them

151
00:08:21,993 --> 00:08:24,366
remember: there are thousand textures

152
00:08:24,366 --> 00:08:26,463
in a real world game project

153
00:08:26,463 --> 00:08:29,793
and how should you manually
choose the right block size?

154
00:08:29,793 --> 00:08:32,013
I suppose you may want to do a test

155
00:08:32,013 --> 00:08:35,103
on the texture using
all block size settings

156
00:08:35,103 --> 00:08:38,373
and finally you can choose
the one that you like.

157
00:08:38,373 --> 00:08:41,403
But, ASTC encoding is a time consuming.

158
00:08:41,403 --> 00:08:42,423
It takes time

159
00:08:42,423 --> 00:08:46,803
and textures will change
rapidly during game development

160
00:08:46,803 --> 00:08:49,836
thus it is impossible for
you to do this manually.

161
00:08:51,363 --> 00:08:53,673
So how should we do it?

162
00:08:53,673 --> 00:08:58,053
First all what we expect is in
the case of ensuring quality

163
00:08:58,053 --> 00:09:01,067
we should choose as large
block size as possible

164
00:09:01,067 --> 00:09:03,786
which means smaller compressed size.

165
00:09:05,193 --> 00:09:07,413
Knowing that manually
checking is impossible

166
00:09:07,413 --> 00:09:10,763
in practice and being
inspired by the success

167
00:09:10,763 --> 00:09:12,803
of machine learning in the real world

168
00:09:12,803 --> 00:09:16,893
or in field or image recognition
and image classification

169
00:09:16,893 --> 00:09:19,743
we decide to use machine
learning to solve this problem.

170
00:09:19,743 --> 00:09:22,653
Because texture compression
is rarely done offline,

171
00:09:22,653 --> 00:09:24,813
we don't need to worry about the size

172
00:09:24,813 --> 00:09:27,276
or execution time or the model we get.

173
00:09:29,583 --> 00:09:30,993
Now the second part.

174
00:09:30,993 --> 00:09:33,903
In order to solve this
problem using machine learning

175
00:09:33,903 --> 00:09:35,946
we first need to model the problem.

176
00:09:39,633 --> 00:09:43,623
Take cat and dog
classification as an example.

177
00:09:43,623 --> 00:09:46,503
Cat and dog classification
is a classical image

178
00:09:46,503 --> 00:09:50,013
classification problem
that is a set pictures

179
00:09:50,013 --> 00:09:51,513
of cats and dogs.

180
00:09:51,513 --> 00:09:53,100
We input them into the model

181
00:09:53,100 --> 00:09:56,193
and the model gives
predictions as to which

182
00:09:56,193 --> 00:09:59,076
as whether this picture is a cat or dog.

183
00:09:59,973 --> 00:10:00,033
To train such a model
we need a set of images

184
00:10:06,093 --> 00:10:08,016
with its right category.

185
00:10:09,003 --> 00:10:10,083
Models learn experience

186
00:10:10,083 --> 00:10:13,206
from this data and that's
how predictive power.

187
00:10:14,073 --> 00:10:17,433
The block size selection
is actually similar.

188
00:10:17,433 --> 00:10:20,703
We want to import the
game texture into a model

189
00:10:20,703 --> 00:10:23,766
and expect the model to
give the best block size.

190
00:10:24,633 --> 00:10:26,583
Now this problem is much simpler.

191
00:10:26,583 --> 00:10:28,833
We just need to build a texture set.

192
00:10:28,833 --> 00:10:32,823
We label them with expected
block size and we train a model,

193
00:10:32,823 --> 00:10:37,173
then we run our model on
textures in our game project.

194
00:10:37,173 --> 00:10:40,593
Hopefully it will tell us
which bulk size should be used

195
00:10:40,593 --> 00:10:41,676
for input texture.

196
00:10:44,523 --> 00:10:48,003
To build a data set, we need
to define a optimization

197
00:10:48,003 --> 00:10:49,503
target first.

198
00:10:49,503 --> 00:10:52,623
In our experiment, our
goal is to make a balance

199
00:10:52,623 --> 00:10:56,673
between compression quality
and compression ratio.

200
00:10:56,673 --> 00:10:59,643
Thus both of them we
have being considered.

201
00:10:59,643 --> 00:11:02,253
For example, we want to make sure that

202
00:11:02,253 --> 00:11:06,783
resulting texture PSNR value
should be about 35 decimal

203
00:11:06,783 --> 00:11:10,263
and the unchanged condition,
the largest block size,

204
00:11:10,263 --> 00:11:11,943
is preferred.

205
00:11:11,943 --> 00:11:14,043
Now we have lost function.

206
00:11:14,043 --> 00:11:15,893
We can start to build our data set.

207
00:11:15,893 --> 00:11:19,773
Before that we make our
baseline to compare waste

208
00:11:19,773 --> 00:11:23,103
in which case our textures
will be compressed using

209
00:11:23,103 --> 00:11:25,626
a default value of six by six.

210
00:11:28,593 --> 00:11:31,533
Building a data set is easy
with the automatization goal

211
00:11:31,533 --> 00:11:35,433
defined for each texture
compresses without the compression

212
00:11:35,433 --> 00:11:39,003
parameters and calculate the PSNR value.

213
00:11:39,003 --> 00:11:41,433
And then we apply the
loss function defined

214
00:11:41,433 --> 00:11:43,923
in the previous page.

215
00:11:43,923 --> 00:11:46,496
We find the most satisfying block size

216
00:11:46,496 --> 00:11:48,703
and use it as a textures label

217
00:11:51,723 --> 00:11:54,461
Repeating the above procedure
for all images we have

218
00:11:54,461 --> 00:11:59,461
we can obtain the data set as
ASTC compression is very slow

219
00:12:00,033 --> 00:12:02,883
and in order to verify our data quickly

220
00:12:02,883 --> 00:12:05,283
we cap the data set Quite small.

221
00:12:05,283 --> 00:12:08,523
Train set has about 10,000 textures

222
00:12:08,523 --> 00:12:11,853
and test set has 2,000 textures.

223
00:12:11,853 --> 00:12:15,393
Now we are halfway down so
far we have the dead set.

224
00:12:15,393 --> 00:12:19,386
Next we need to have a
classification network.

225
00:12:22,353 --> 00:12:25,728
We use a deep convolutional
network with the residual block

226
00:12:25,728 --> 00:12:28,263
with the residual block structure

227
00:12:28,263 --> 00:12:31,623
in the convolutional layer
where each residual block adds

228
00:12:31,623 --> 00:12:32,943
up the original data

229
00:12:32,943 --> 00:12:35,670
and the data passed through
the convolutional block

230
00:12:35,670 --> 00:12:38,856
which is experimentally
proven to be quite good.

231
00:12:40,143 --> 00:12:43,323
In fact there are so many
models to choose from

232
00:12:43,323 --> 00:12:44,193
but we believe most

233
00:12:44,193 --> 00:12:47,883
of them will have this job
done cause it is very simple.

234
00:12:47,883 --> 00:12:49,713
So there's no need to focus too much

235
00:12:49,713 --> 00:12:51,723
on the model we choose, let's focus

236
00:12:51,723 --> 00:12:53,346
on the training results instead.

237
00:12:55,563 --> 00:12:57,636
We will summarize whatever you have got.

238
00:12:58,584 --> 00:13:01,263
First of all, the distribution

239
00:13:01,263 --> 00:13:04,173
of block size chosen by our model.

240
00:13:04,173 --> 00:13:06,393
As you can see that on the test set

241
00:13:06,393 --> 00:13:09,483
the model choose six by six for about four

242
00:13:09,483 --> 00:13:12,933
about 40% of the textures
which is consistent

243
00:13:12,933 --> 00:13:16,008
with the default value.
For other textures,

244
00:13:16,008 --> 00:13:19,563
a larger compression parameter is selected

245
00:13:19,563 --> 00:13:22,683
among which 12 by 12 is the most.

246
00:13:22,683 --> 00:13:25,413
As we have mentioned, 12 by 12 only takes

247
00:13:25,413 --> 00:13:28,743
up a quarter of what six by six takes.

248
00:13:28,743 --> 00:13:30,783
So after compressing,

249
00:13:30,783 --> 00:13:33,303
with the parameter selected by the model,

250
00:13:33,303 --> 00:13:37,563
the size of the compressed
set is significantly reduced.

251
00:13:37,563 --> 00:13:41,193
It is not enough to just
look at the compressed size.

252
00:13:41,193 --> 00:13:43,763
We also need to focus on the quality

253
00:13:43,763 --> 00:13:45,366
of the compressed image.

254
00:13:47,793 --> 00:13:49,699
Here shows some of the commercial results

255
00:13:49,699 --> 00:13:51,813
for each power textures.

256
